- F-336 **A compiled helper's raise builds the exception of the library in use, with its message where the exception has a constructor over that library's `String`.** `Utility.makeFortressException(name, msg)`, and `(name, e)` through it, takes the exception's class by the name that the class path's `LibraryNames` gives the exception's simple name, which the helper passes: the prelude's class, or under the selection the one library's (F-396). It passes the message as an `FJavaString` to the exception's constructor over that library's `String`. An exception without that constructor, as the one library's `IOFailure` and `FileNotFoundException`, is made by its constructor without parameters, without the message (`compiler/runtimeValues/Utility.java:34-52`), and so is a second instance of the object (row 1103). So on the compiled path a helper's `FailCalled`, `FileNotFoundException` and `IOFailure` reach a `catch` against the prelude, and its `IntegerOverflow` and `FileNotFoundException` against the one library; under walk a helper's raise still becomes the library's object of the same name, without the message (F-299). Tests: `compiler_tests/HelperRaiseMessageCompiled`, `one_library_tests/OneLibraryCatchHelperRaises.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-runtime-threads/REPORT.md`, section 1; `compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md`, "The design as built (a point to report)".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md, "The design as built (a point to report)"; from: reviews/batch-34b-review.md, "1. The landed change against the decisions, path by path"; commit: 295c215c2; note: the title claimed that every raise builds the exception over the library's String, where an exception without that constructor is made without the message (Utility.java:49-51); the two libraries' constructor lookup put in one sentence, the two String class names left to the report; row 1103 kept to its own scope, an exception object without value parameters; the two lists of caught exceptions named by their library

- F-353 **One build can serve the compiled path with either library, shown in a scratch tree: the prelude's thirteen run-time value classes stay, and a second set under the same names, in a second class folder that the class path puts first, serves the one library.** The scratch tree is that of F-352 with its hacks, nine of them behind a selector that one environment variable sets at the compile and at the run. One JVM serves one library. There the one-line program runs against both libraries, and the `Array1` program and the dot loop against the one library, by hand and through `fortress junit`. A sample of 137 of the 876 files of `compiler_tests` shows no test that the second set disturbed; the whole compiled tracks and walk were not run. There the second set's sources equal the first's under a map of eight names, since the classes then differed only in the generated classes that they extend. There the classes of the build that link against a prelude class by name are the thirteen, `FValue`, `MutableFValue`, `Utility`, `FException`, `FZZ32Vector` and `FStringVector`; every other mention of the prelude in the run time and the helpers is a string, 105 raise strings among them (read in the constant pools of `ProjectFortress/build`); on `main` a raise string names the exception by its simple name (F-336). `main` has its own selector (F-381) and builds such a second set, by the same mechanism with files of its own (F-396); its seven number types need no second set, since their payload classes over `FValue` serve the object classes that the code generator writes for either library (F-388), except for the `asString` bridge that compiled generic code calls, typed at the prelude's `String` (row 1101). The scratch tree's hacks did not land as such, and no test holds its runs. Detail: `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/probe-y.md`, "1. What ran", "2. How the library is selected" and "5. What the second set costs".
