- F-381 **The compile command reads the one library where `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one` (or `-Dfortress.library=one`) selects it, keeps its caches in `default_repository/caches-one`, and its tests run in `testFast`'s onelibrary track.** The selection is read once per JVM (`ProjectProperties.compiledPathUsesOneLibrary`); `Shell.useCompilerLibraries()` follows it, and the two class path scripts follow the variable only. Unset, every command reads the prelude as before. A program compiled under the selection can run, as one that prints a line of text does, against the one library's jars, which the onelibrary track compiles first (F-396). Tests: `ProjectFortress/one_library_tests/OneLibraryHalf.test`, `OneLibraryPrintBig.test`, `OneLibraryRunHello.test` and `XXXOneLibraryRunHalf.test`, run by `ant testOneLibrary` and by `ant testFast`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-selector/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why", "The tests: the failing run on the base, the passing run" and "Wrong pairings, and the run under the selection"; `compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md`, "The tests: the failing runs on the base, the passing run".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md, "The tests: the failing runs on the base, the passing run"; commit: 295c215c2; note: the source of the run sentence, written by climb batch 34b's gather, added to the Detail

- F-388 **The compiler's prelude declares its seven number types as value objects, whose classes the code generator writes over the run time's payload classes.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `ZZ`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `RR32` and `RR64` are `value object`s in `CompilerBuiltin`; each object's class extends the abstract payload class `compiler/runtimeValues/F<T>`, which holds the JVM value. Descriptors, stamped instances and the RTTI's `className()` name the type by the payload class, while the RTTI's class stays the object's, so that the type lies below its traits at run time; a dotted call casts its receiver to the object's class; `F<T>.make` reaches the object's class's `make` by the name that `LibraryNames` gives: the first set's names the prelude's seven, and under the selection the code generator writes the one library's seven over the same payload classes, whose names the second set's `LibraryNames` gives (F-396). So a program's object below a number type is refused at the compile, an overloading at `ZZ32` beside one at a program's trait is valid, a `typecase` clause at such a trait over a number is refused as unreachable (row 1081), and a number type named as a value does not compile (row 1082). Tests: `compiler_tests/NumberObjectsCompiled.test`, `compiler_tests/XXXNumberTypeExtendRefusedCompiled.test`, `compiler_tests/ComprisesMeetGenericTrait.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-number-payload-objects/REPORT.md`, "The design as built (a point to report)"; `compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md`, "The design as built (a point to report)".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md, "The design as built (a point to report)"; commit: 295c215c2; note: the source of the one library's seven over the payload classes, written by climb batch 34b's gather, added to the Detail

- F-390 **The code generator passes a varargs parameter's arguments as one `ImmutableArray[\T, ZZ32\]` of the library in use; against the compiler's prelude, which declares no such type, it refuses the declaration.** The varargs part of a domain is one JVM parameter after the plain ones (`NamingCzar.varargsParamType`). The array is made at the call, or in a dispatch method's branch where another declaration of the name takes as many arguments, and is filled in order with `init`, as walk makes it (`CodeGen.evalVarargsArgs`, `emitVarargsArray`); a single argument of tuple type passes its elements. A call reaches the declaration by the specification's expansion rule (`OverloadSet.VarargsExpansion`). Under the selection of the one library (F-381) the code generator still stops on a varargs method, or a varargs function with static parameters, beside another declaration of its name with as many parameters (row 1086); a spread argument, `f(1, ys...)`, is dropped without a message on both paths (row 1085). So under the selection the library's varargs declarations that a program reaches, such as `println` and `assert`, compile. There the index of each element, made by `FZZ32.make`, agrees with the array's `init`, since the one library's `ZZ32` is `FZZ32` in every descriptor (F-388, F-396); with the JVM's verifier on, the array's class does not load, since it holds `ImmutableArray1`'s `copy` twice (row 1091), so no program that calls a varargs declaration runs yet. Tests: the five `one_library_tests/OneLibraryVarargs*.test` and `XXXOneLibraryRunVarargs.test`; the prelude's `compiler_tests/XXXVarargsCodeGeneration.test` and `XXXVarargsMethodCodeGeneration.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-varargs/REPORT.md`, "The convention as built"; `compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md`, "The run tests (a point to report)"; `reviews/batch-33b-review.md`, section 2, G.worker.1.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md, "The run tests (a point to report)"; from: reviews/batch-34b-review.md, "1. The landed change against the decisions, path by path"; commit: 295c215c2; note: the run's account cut to the wall and its row; the index agreement with init kept (FZZ32.make at CodeGen.java:8014-8015, :4676-4677, :4717-4718); the read-back with the verifier off, the scratch run that printed 7 and 9, goes to the report

- F-396 **The one library compiles to jars over a second set of run-time classes, and a program compiled against them can run on the compiled path, as one that prints a line does.** Under `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one` the onelibrary track compiles the one library's fourteen components first, in one `.test` (`OneLibraryJUTest.java:44`), and a program compiled against them runs with the JVM's verifier on, as `Hello` does. The run time's classes that name a library's types come in two sets under one set of names: `ProjectFortress/build` for the prelude, and `ProjectFortress/build-one`, built from `ProjectFortress/src-one` by `ant compileAll`, for the one library, which the class path puts first under the selection. The one library's seven number types, `Boolean`, `Char` and `FlatString` are objects whose classes the code generator writes over payload classes (`NamingCzar.java:637-655`), all value objects but `FlatString`, a reference object (`Library/FlatString.fss:32`), and a helper's raise names its exception by its simple name, which each set's `LibraryNames` maps to its library's class. Against the one library the compiled path still departs from walk, each at a row: a varargs call (row 1091); a reduction over a range (row 1099); `println` of a number or a boolean, which the export check's rule decides as much as the code generator (row 1098); `=` of two strings or two booleans, and `<` of two strings (row 994); `u.asString` in a generic body at `U extends ZZ32` (row 1101); the report of an uncaught exception (row 1102); a caught exception that a helper raises, which is not its object (row 1103). The library's compile depends on what its JVM compiled before (row 1100), so the track compiles it first. Tests: `one_library_tests/OneLibraryComponents.test`, `OneLibraryRunHello.test`, `OneLibraryCatchHelperRaises.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md`, "The design as built (a point to report)".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md, "The design as built (a point to report)"; from: reviews/batch-34b-review.md, "1. The landed change against the decisions, path by path"; commit: 295c215c2; note: the walls named as the departures from walk that the review lists, rows 1101, 1102 and 1103 added and row 1098 named as the export rule's too, where the text named "the code generator's own walls" and four rows; the title cut to what the body shows, "can run ... as one that prints a line does", where it said "runs on the compiled path"; the second set's stamp (build.xml:402), bin/fortress_classpath:27-39 and the track's classesFirst left to the report

- F-24 **Under `walk`, a native can raise a Fortress exception that a Fortress `catch` sees.** It throws a `FortressError` that holds the exception's object, as `ZZ32.overflow()` does for `IntegerOverflow` (F-132; row 347, fixed). A native bound to a helper of `nativeHelpers/` raises the library's own exception where the helper raises one by its simple name, such as `DivisionByZero`, since walk reads that name back from the class name that the class path's `LibraryNames` gives the simple name (F-336), so `DIV`, `REM` and `MOD` by zero on `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `ZZ`, `NN32` and `NN64` raise `DivisionByZero` (F-299, F-306, F-308, F-312). Any other Java exception of a native becomes a `ProgramError`, which no `catch` takes. Gated by the `overflows(...)` assertions of `tests/IntSemanticsRungI.fss`, and for a helper by `tests/NativeHelperRaiseWalk.fss` and, for each of the five types, `tests/<type>DivisionByZeroWalk.fss` and `tests/<type>RemainderByZeroWalk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-int-semantics-walk/REPORT.md` section 4.1.
  from: reviews/batch-34b-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; commit: 295c215c2; note: false since climb batch 34b's rung C, which made a helper name its exception by its simple name (nativeHelpers/simpleIntArith.java:43, Utility.java:34-36, StaticNativeApp.raised at :273-282); the old words "where the helper raises a class of the compiled prelude, such as fortress.CompilerBuiltin$DivisionByZero"; walk's answers unchanged

- F-312 **`REM` and `MOD` on `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64` and `ZZ` name helpers of `nativeHelpers/` on both paths, keep every value the glue classes gave, and throw `DivisionByZero` at a zero divisor.** The ten bindings (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:910-913`, `:1022-1025`, `:1130-1133`, `:1248-1251`; `ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:328-331`) name `simpleIntArith.intRem` and `intMod`, `simpleLongArith.longRem` and `longMod`, `simpleUnsignedIntArith.unsignedIntRem` and `unsignedIntMod`, `simpleUnsignedLongArith.unsignedLongRem` and `unsignedLongMod` (through the transfers of F-308), and `simpleArbitraryPrecisionArith.rem` and `mod`: `REM` the remainder of the truncating division, `MOD` of the floor division with the divisor's sign, and on the natural numbers `MOD` the same as `REM`. At a zero divisor each raises `DivisionByZero` by its simple name, which the class path's `LibraryNames` maps to the compiled library's class (F-336), and walk raises the library's `DivisionByZero` (F-24). 626 printed pairs are the old code's. Test: `tests/ZZ32RemainderByZeroWalk`, `ZZ64RemainderByZeroWalk`, `NN32RemainderByZeroWalk`, `NN64RemainderByZeroWalk`, `ZZRemainderByZeroWalk`, `NativeHelperFamilyRemainderWalk`; `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyRemainderCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-remainders/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-34b-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; commit: 295c215c2; note: false since climb batch 34b's rung C, which made a helper name its exception by its simple name; the old words "Each raises fortress.CompilerBuiltin$DivisionByZero at a zero divisor, which walk raises as the library's DivisionByZero (F-24)"

- F-332 **Walk carries `FortressLibrary`'s `JavaBufferedReader` in a Java `FortressBufferedReader`, and `FileReadStream`, `Reader`, `stdIn` and `FileWriteStream` are plain Fortress objects over the Java-backed traits `JavaBufferedReader` and `JavaBufferedWriter`.** The trait's eight methods and its two factories, over a file name and for standard input, are natives on `nativeHelpers/fileOps` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2083-2103`); walk's loader carries the trait by its name through `FortressBuiltin`'s private native object `JavaBufferedReaderObject` (`interpreter/glue/StaticNativeApp.java:71`). `File` and `Reader` are plain components, and each object holds its Java-backed value in its field `under`. `uncheckedReadChar` answers -1 at the end by the reader's `eof`, since `jbrRead` answers 0 there. `FileWriteStream` is a `WriteStream` with its `flush`. A missing file raises `FileNotFoundException`, and a read after `consume` raises `IOFailure`. A `catch` sees each on both paths, since a compiled helper's raise builds the exception of the library in use (F-336). F-299 counts the bindings. Tests: `tests/NativeHelperReaderWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyReaderCompiled`, `compiler_tests/FileOpsRaiseCaughtCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-io-reader/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-34b-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; commit: 295c215c2; note: false since climb batch 34b's rung C, under which the compiled one library's raise builds the one library's exception (one_library_tests/OneLibraryCatchHelperRaises.test catches a helper's FileNotFoundException); the old words "builds the prelude's exception"

- F-301 **On the compiled path a native helper is called through a generated wrapper over the box classes, and once the loop is hot C2 inlines the whole path, so the call costs nothing of its own.** A binding `builtinPrimitive("pkg.Class.method")` and a written `import java` alias call compile to the same bytes: an `invokestatic` of `native.pkg.Class.method`, a class the compiled path writes into `nativewrapper_cache/`, which unboxes each argument with `getValue`, calls the helper and boxes its result with `make`, a new box each time (`compiler/nativeInterface/FortressMethodAdapter.java:303-327`; `compiler/runtimeValues/FZZ32.java:40-48`, where `make` now calls the `make` of the number's value object, the prelude's or under the selection the one library's, through a method handle, F-388). When the prelude declared its number types as traits, a functional method such as `ZZ32`'s `+` reached the wrapper through the dispatcher, an interface call, the springboard and the trait's static body. C2 inlines all of these frames, the helper, `make` and the box's constructor chain. Escape analysis then removes every temporary box, a helper's result passed into another helper among them, and one box stays per assignment to a mutable variable per iteration: 16 bytes for a `ZZ32`, 24 for an `RR64`. The library's form and the prelude's allocate the same. Observed once, while the prelude declared them as traits, and not since they are value objects, on one small program, `perf-probes/helper-calls/HelperCalls.fss`, by `javap`, C2's inlining log and the bytes that JFR counted, with no timing, on a machine shared at a load of 20 to 25; no test checks it. Detail: `perf-probes/helper-calls/REPORT.md` sections 2 to 5.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md, "The design as built (a point to report)"; commit: 295c215c2; note: since climb batch 34b's rung C the handle reaches the one library's value object under the selection, through the second set's LibraryNames (F-388); the old words "the make of the prelude's value object"; FZZ32.java:40-48 checked at 295c215c2 and unchanged; the measurement, taken against the prelude, stays

